---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LegalNav from '../components/LegalNav.astro';
import { SUPPORT_EMAIL } from '../config/site';

const description =
  'Appidge data usage: what stays local, what the app sends to activate a license or run the 7-day trial, update checks, and the built-in forced routing rules (self/loopback exclusion, Apple signing infrastructure direct-connect, upstream pass-through).';
---

<BaseLayout title="Data Usage" description={description} path="/data-usage" locale="en">
  <div class="doc">
    <div class="container doc-layout">
      <LegalNav locale="en" current="data-usage" />
      <article class="prose doc-main">
        <h1>Data Usage</h1>
        <div class="doc-meta">
          <span><b>Effective</b> 2026-07-24</span>
          <span><b>Last updated</b> 2026-07-24</span>
          <span><b>Covers</b> the appidge.com website and the Appidge macOS app</span>
        </div>

        <p class="lede">
          A plain-language account of what data Appidge touches. Short version:
          <strong>your rules and traffic stay on your Mac; the only things that leave it are
          license checks and update checks</strong>, and this page lists exactly what those are.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">1</span>The website</h2>
        <p>
          appidge.com is a fully static site: <strong>no cookies, no third-party tracking, no
          profiling</strong>. Our host (Cloudflare) may briefly process access logs (such as IP
          addresses) under its own policies to serve and protect the site; we don't use those
          logs to identify you.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">2</span>What stays on your Mac</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your rules, the list of intercepted processes, and per-connection details are stored
            and processed <strong>locally only</strong> — they are never uploaded to us.</li>
          <li>Your network traffic is forwarded on your Mac to the proxy you configured, or
            directly. <strong>It never passes through our servers.</strong></li>
          <li>There is no built-in crash reporting or usage analytics. If that ever changes, it
            will be opt-in and this page will be updated first.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2><span class="num">3</span>License activation &amp; the 7-day trial</h2>
        <p>
          When you activate or validate a license, the app sends our licensing service only what
          the check requires:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>License key</td>
                <td>Verify the purchase</td>
                <td>Recorded server-side in redacted form; used for nothing else</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Install identifier</td>
                <td>Count activated devices</td>
                <td>A privacy-friendly random value — <strong>not</strong> your email, hostname,
                  or hardware serial number</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>App version</td>
                <td>Compatibility</td>
                <td>—</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The <strong>7-day trial is tracked entirely on your Mac</strong> — the first-launch time
          lives in the macOS Keychain and a local file, and is <strong>not sent to any
          server</strong>. License data (key, install identifier, last check time) is stored in
          the Keychain, never in plain-text files. Payment data is handled by Creem, the Merchant
          of Record — see the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">4</span>Update checks</h2>
        <p>
          The app periodically asks our update server (<code>updates.appidge.com</code>) for new
          versions via Sparkle. The request returns update information and contains nothing that
          identifies you personally; server access logs are handled as in section 1. Automatic
          checks can be turned off in the app's settings.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">5</span>Built-in routing rules we apply for you</h2>
        <p>
          For correctness and reliability, a few connections are always routed a fixed way,
          <strong>regardless of your rules</strong>. In the interest of transparency, here they
          are in full:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Appidge itself and proxy loopback are excluded</strong> — the app's own
            traffic and your proxy client's own outbound are passed through directly, so nothing
            loops back on itself and breaks your connection.</li>
          <li><strong>Apple signing &amp; notarization infrastructure is forced direct</strong> —
            six Apple domains (<code>timestamp.apple.com</code>, <code>ocsp.apple.com</code>,
            <code>ocsp2.apple.com</code>, <code>crl.apple.com</code>, <code>valid.apple.com</code>,
            <code>appstoreconnect.apple.com</code>) always connect directly. These handle code
            signing timestamps and certificate checks that break when sent through a flaky proxy;
            forcing them direct keeps signing and Gatekeeper working. It does not affect other
            Apple domains — <code>www.apple.com</code> and the like still follow your rules.</li>
          <li><strong>The update channel goes direct</strong> — downloads from
            <code>updates.appidge.com</code> don't pass through the licensing service.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These built-in rules exist purely to keep the software working; none of them send any
          additional data anywhere.
        </p>

        <h2><span class="num">6</span>Your rights &amp; contact</h2>
        <p>
          Under applicable law you may request access to, correction of, or deletion of data we
          hold about you — email <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>. Requests
          about payment data may also need to go to Creem; we'll help you connect. Material
          changes to this page will be posted here with a new effective date.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
